[{:db/id 17592186110776 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-334188d1-1448-5a61-bc9c-b781073c37c1" "question-ef405c5c-f229-5ca4-acda-70f35ff263e9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110405 :question/problem "Which of the following terms describe the shape below?\n\nI. Parallelogram\n\nII. Rectangle\n\nIII. Square\n\n![](images/d5/d578ca7af1f030dabb8e44a5a8fab90ce4bd21d002054fccdf2be2413a855a5c.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at the terms one by one.\n\n- The given figure is a quadrilateral with two pairs of opposite congruent angles, so it is a parallelogram.\n\n- Since all the angles in this parallelogram are right, it is a rectangle.\n\n- Not all the sides of this rectangle are congruent, so it is not a square.\n\nConsequently, the given shape is described by terms I (parallelogram) and II (rectangle)." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110777 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d149b00e-3076-56e3-b5b0-ca0e159fc928" "question-e6a1177b-8e6f-5855-8414-02d987aa4107"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110406 :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a trapezoid is a quadrilateral with exactly one pair of opposite sides that are parallel to each other.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $\\mathcal{P}$ is a trapezoid since it has one pair of opposite sides that are parallel.\n\n- The shape $\\mathcal{Q}$ is a rhombus. It has two pairs of opposite sides that are parallel to one another, not one pair. Therefore, it is not a trapezoid.\n\n- The shape $\\mathcal{R}$ is a kite. Its opposite sides are not parallel, therefore it is not a trapezoid.\n\n- The shape $\\mathcal{S}$ is a rectangle. It has two pairs of opposite sides that are parallel to one another, not one pair. Therefore, it is not a trapezoid.\n\nTherefore, only shape $\\mathcal{P}$ is a trapezoid." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110778 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8fe04944-0102-5a76-9a34-b22553e0ce91"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110408 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. A rhombus is always a kite.\n\nII. A rhombus is always a parallelogram.\n\nIII. A kite is always a parallelogram." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. A kite has two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other. A rhombus fits this description, because all the sides in a rhombus are congruent.\n\n- Statement II is true. In a parallelogram, opposite sides are parallel. The same is true for a rhombus. A rhombus is a specific type of parallelogram, where all the sides are congruent.\n\n- Statement III is false.  In a parallelogram, opposite sides are parallel. However, in a kite, it's only necessary that two pairs of congruent sides are adjacent to each other. Therefore, a kite is not always a parallelogram.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111260 :question/problem "![](images/6b/6bffbc53c9e35da03655638ca40c5899bc5290cbadf3d14aef8e82585f14e004.png)\n\nWhich of the following terms describe the shape above?\n\nI. Kite\n\nII. Rectangle\n\nIII. Trapezoid" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at the terms one by one.\n\n- The given figure is a quadrilateral with two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other, so it is a kite.\n\n- Since the four angles of the quadrilateral are right angles, it is a rectangle.\n\n- The quadrilateral has two pairs of opposite sides that are parallel to each other, not one pair. So it is **not** a trapezoid.\n\nConsequently, the given shape is described by terms I (kite), and II (rectangle)." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186109285 :answer-field/correct 17592186109287 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-334188d1-1448-5a61-bc9c-b781073c37c1" :question/id #uuid "334188d1-1448-5a61-bc9c-b781073c37c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-74510" :question/problem "![](images/cc/cc3e39478d45abcbd97722616961adcd3a10aaa9da78845b22ea9566b324fa34.png)\n\nWhich of the following terms describe the shape above?\n\nI. Square\n\nII. Rhombus\n\nIII. Parallelogram" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at the terms one by one.\n\n- The given figure is a quadrilateral with angles that are not all right. So it is **not** a square.\n\n- Since the four sides of the quadrilateral are congruent, it is a rhombus.\n\n- The quadrilateral is a rhombus. A rhombus is a parallelogram where all four sides are equal. So it is a parallelogram.\n\nConsequently, the given shape is described by terms II (rhombus), and III (parallelogram)." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8dd86c5b-5668-52b2-9022-45e2df077d5e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8dd86c5b-5668-52b2-9022-45e2df077d5e" :answer-field/id #uuid "8dd86c5b-5668-52b2-9022-45e2df077d5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f67cbff6-b72d-5a9e-b1b3-7aa59a9ce1f0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e8c9ddeb-40d8-5c69-bdf1-cf098a254efd" "answer-08dad930-3bec-5023-9f09-d5e37c38df03" "answer-937c2af6-40ad-55ca-b87d-3c33e8fb38c9" "answer-f67cbff6-b72d-5a9e-b1b3-7aa59a9ce1f0" "answer-2793e408-578a-5a06-b01c-25af3c2f51ba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8c9ddeb-40d8-5c69-bdf1-cf098a254efd" :answer/id #uuid "e8c9ddeb-40d8-5c69-bdf1-cf098a254efd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08dad930-3bec-5023-9f09-d5e37c38df03" :answer/id #uuid "08dad930-3bec-5023-9f09-d5e37c38df03" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-937c2af6-40ad-55ca-b87d-3c33e8fb38c9" :answer/id #uuid "937c2af6-40ad-55ca-b87d-3c33e8fb38c9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f67cbff6-b72d-5a9e-b1b3-7aa59a9ce1f0" :answer/id #uuid "f67cbff6-b72d-5a9e-b1b3-7aa59a9ce1f0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2793e408-578a-5a06-b01c-25af3c2f51ba" :answer/id #uuid "2793e408-578a-5a06-b01c-25af3c2f51ba" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ef405c5c-f229-5ca4-acda-70f35ff263e9" :question/id #uuid "ef405c5c-f229-5ca4-acda-70f35ff263e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-128157" :question/problem "![](images/da/da2336d6c4360d10fedbfc6441280b5a08091df34f3f59e2e94e7d4daf01bc3f.png)\n\nWhich of the following terms describe the shape above?\n\nI. Square\n\nII. Trapezoid\n\nIII. Rhombus" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at the terms one by one.\n\n- The given figure is a quadrilateral where all sides are congruent and all angles are right, so it is a square.\n\n- Since the quadrilateral has two pairs of opposite sides that are parallel to each other, not one pair, it is **not** a trapezoid.\n\n- The quadrilateral is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent. So it is a rhombus.\n\nConsequently, the given shape is described by terms I (square)  and III (rhombus)." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6d5d2df3-6e2f-5d97-b571-87478c1a4929"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6d5d2df3-6e2f-5d97-b571-87478c1a4929" :answer-field/id #uuid "6d5d2df3-6e2f-5d97-b571-87478c1a4929" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9b65575c-de89-5d3b-a864-c1c86a19a697" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f4fbe29d-ac6e-50df-9af8-75419d3d96e0" "answer-9b65575c-de89-5d3b-a864-c1c86a19a697" "answer-274c9f91-07ea-5d83-8c64-64d15923d767" "answer-d7345b8a-0546-52d8-b568-a7582dd09262" "answer-2cc4133d-8cbc-54e2-9bb1-8bef4ee6f18e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4fbe29d-ac6e-50df-9af8-75419d3d96e0" :answer/id #uuid "f4fbe29d-ac6e-50df-9af8-75419d3d96e0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b65575c-de89-5d3b-a864-c1c86a19a697" :answer/id #uuid "9b65575c-de89-5d3b-a864-c1c86a19a697" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-274c9f91-07ea-5d83-8c64-64d15923d767" :answer/id #uuid "274c9f91-07ea-5d83-8c64-64d15923d767" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7345b8a-0546-52d8-b568-a7582dd09262" :answer/id #uuid "d7345b8a-0546-52d8-b568-a7582dd09262" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2cc4133d-8cbc-54e2-9bb1-8bef4ee6f18e" :answer/id #uuid "2cc4133d-8cbc-54e2-9bb1-8bef4ee6f18e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d149b00e-3076-56e3-b5b0-ca0e159fc928" :question/id #uuid "d149b00e-3076-56e3-b5b0-ca0e159fc928" :question/math-academy-id "q-128291" :question/problem "![](images/94/94a6607f6d12c3c59c9822d91dfb6c6b8b3a8d23bba9ef1c3a7253be564f95d8.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a parallelogram?" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a parallelogram is a quadrilateral with $2$ pairs of parallel sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is an isosceles trapezoid. There is a pair of opposite sides that are not parallel, therefore, it is not a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a square. Therefore, its opposite sides are parallel, so it is a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a rhombus. Since every pair of opposite sides in a rhombus are parallel, it is a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $S$ is a kite in which no pair of opposite sides are parallel, so it is not a parallelogram.\n\nTherefore, shapes $Q$ and $R$ are parallelograms. The shapes $P$ and $S$ are not parallelograms." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-25a7aabb-2b7a-53d0-b50c-ce5b5db78d9d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-25a7aabb-2b7a-53d0-b50c-ce5b5db78d9d" :answer-field/id #uuid "25a7aabb-2b7a-53d0-b50c-ce5b5db78d9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-472d4181-0400-5371-9918-356dcb9272a2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8c5d7662-6751-5ba8-a72b-f701f02b9659" "answer-460e7f6c-aa47-56f6-b331-546223765a0d" "answer-472d4181-0400-5371-9918-356dcb9272a2" "answer-ba482929-e458-5616-88b5-17d64aa90840" "answer-d95ed278-9540-5cb0-91d2-fa17ecfaa247"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c5d7662-6751-5ba8-a72b-f701f02b9659" :answer/id #uuid "8c5d7662-6751-5ba8-a72b-f701f02b9659" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$P$  only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-460e7f6c-aa47-56f6-b331-546223765a0d" :answer/id #uuid "460e7f6c-aa47-56f6-b331-546223765a0d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$P$ and $S$  only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-472d4181-0400-5371-9918-356dcb9272a2" :answer/id #uuid "472d4181-0400-5371-9918-356dcb9272a2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$  only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba482929-e458-5616-88b5-17d64aa90840" :answer/id #uuid "ba482929-e458-5616-88b5-17d64aa90840" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d95ed278-9540-5cb0-91d2-fa17ecfaa247" :answer/id #uuid "d95ed278-9540-5cb0-91d2-fa17ecfaa247" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$S$  only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e6a1177b-8e6f-5855-8414-02d987aa4107" :question/id #uuid "e6a1177b-8e6f-5855-8414-02d987aa4107" :question/math-academy-id "q-128251" :question/problem "![](images/78/781e1e9f5a08cf7f01d9896e957f8d983cd428817e3a5eeb90312a89fdc0a932.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a rhombus?" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a  rhombus is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent. The opposite angles of a rhombus are also congruent.  \n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is a rectangle. The sides are not all congruent, so it is not a rhombus.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a kite. Since the sides are not all congruent, it is not a rhombus.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a rhombus since all its sides are congruent and the opposite sides are parallel.\n\n- The shape $S$ is an isosceles trapezoid.  Since it has only one pair of congruent sides, it is not a rhombus.\n\nTherefore, only shape $R$ is a rhombus." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c1134a5b-29fb-5dbe-a5b8-c1fc4bb904c9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c1134a5b-29fb-5dbe-a5b8-c1fc4bb904c9" :answer-field/id #uuid "c1134a5b-29fb-5dbe-a5b8-c1fc4bb904c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6c8f7685-3066-517f-a583-76db88d6e75a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9d834235-9a54-5464-b64a-7b2cad018124" "answer-50412497-1363-5a7a-bc67-c737bddc5db6" "answer-e4c89198-c72f-537e-aef1-1c2295f53343" "answer-83868f10-611f-563b-8743-8cf6e8a7dedd" "answer-6c8f7685-3066-517f-a583-76db88d6e75a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d834235-9a54-5464-b64a-7b2cad018124" :answer/id #uuid "9d834235-9a54-5464-b64a-7b2cad018124" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50412497-1363-5a7a-bc67-c737bddc5db6" :answer/id #uuid "50412497-1363-5a7a-bc67-c737bddc5db6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Q$ and $S$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4c89198-c72f-537e-aef1-1c2295f53343" :answer/id #uuid "e4c89198-c72f-537e-aef1-1c2295f53343" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83868f10-611f-563b-8743-8cf6e8a7dedd" :answer/id #uuid "83868f10-611f-563b-8743-8cf6e8a7dedd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$R$ and $Q$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c8f7685-3066-517f-a583-76db88d6e75a" :answer/id #uuid "6c8f7685-3066-517f-a583-76db88d6e75a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8fe04944-0102-5a76-9a34-b22553e0ce91" :question/id #uuid "8fe04944-0102-5a76-9a34-b22553e0ce91" :question/math-academy-id "q-53060" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. A square is always a kite.\n\nII. A rectangle is always a square.\n\nIII. A rhombus is always a trapezoid." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](images/1d/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. A kite has two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other. A square fits the description because all the sides in a square are congruent.\n\n- Statement II is false. In a square, all the sides are congruent. However, in a rectangle, it's only necessary that two pairs of opposite sides are congruent to each other. Therefore, a rectangle is not always a square.\n\n- Statement III is false. In a trapezoid, only $1$ pair of opposite sides are parallel to each other. However, in a rhombus, the $2$ pairs of opposite sides are parallel to each other. Therefore, a rhombus is not a trapezoid.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a86d07c0-4185-50bb-bf78-3ee1ddde8cc1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a86d07c0-4185-50bb-bf78-3ee1ddde8cc1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a86d07c0-4185-50bb-bf78-3ee1ddde8cc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5490e93b-2535-5618-b775-2b269a4b4815" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b9056c57-907f-5eec-9a56-d31436b0f16c" "answer-5490e93b-2535-5618-b775-2b269a4b4815" "answer-74c6cb69-f0a7-5ef4-9ddd-04b2362f40f1" "answer-e87d5322-2b09-569e-af79-49e11b59bcb0" "answer-85e91a34-837c-56d1-8278-c12f595fa8fc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9056c57-907f-5eec-9a56-d31436b0f16c" :answer/id #uuid "b9056c57-907f-5eec-9a56-d31436b0f16c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5490e93b-2535-5618-b775-2b269a4b4815" :answer/id #uuid "5490e93b-2535-5618-b775-2b269a4b4815" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74c6cb69-f0a7-5ef4-9ddd-04b2362f40f1" :answer/id #uuid "74c6cb69-f0a7-5ef4-9ddd-04b2362f40f1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e87d5322-2b09-569e-af79-49e11b59bcb0" :answer/id #uuid "e87d5322-2b09-569e-af79-49e11b59bcb0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85e91a34-837c-56d1-8278-c12f595fa8fc" :answer/id #uuid "85e91a34-837c-56d1-8278-c12f595fa8fc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111262 :question/problem "![](images/b6/b68f8d3d0634999697051b668c06dd34c1191905a7bebda79925cd437d454d36.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the trapezoid above?\n\nI. $\\overset{―}{AB}∥\\overset{―}{CD}.$\n\nII. $m∠ABC={90}^{∘}.$\n\nIII. $\\overset{―}{AD}∥\\overset{―}{BC}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The trapezoid has a pair of parallel sides, $\\overset{―}{AB}∥\\overset{―}{CD}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. We can see that $∠ABC$ is a right angle, that is, $m∠ABC={90}^{∘}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The trapezoid has one pair of parallel sides, $\\overset{―}{AB}∥\\overset{―}{CD}.$ We can see that $\\overset{―}{AD}$ and $\\overset{―}{BC}$ are not parallel.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I and II are true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186109297 :answer-field/correct 17592186109298 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186111845 :tutorial/content "There are lots of different types of quadrilaterals. Let's look at some examples.\n\n- A **trapezoid** is a quadrilateral with exactly ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) pair of opposite sides that are parallel to each other. \nThe parallel sides are called the **bases**. The other two sides are called the **legs**.\n\n![](images/9f/9fa09c46a2119d35c796065a696954931e20062fd5b3677a5484bef92bc2b0d8.png)\n\n- An **isosceles trapezoid** is a  trapezoid with congruent legs.\n\n![](images/b6/b6b2b2012c1d724f9da8b23551ec4f978f190d86875d7db7a3074635d7f40c02.png)\n\n- A **parallelogram** is a quadrilateral with ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of parallel sides.\n\n \n\n![](images/5d/5d0f383f3510f54b415a7ab99ef52fb30270924f97b8afec59534a96cd3edcef.png)\n\n Opposite sides and opposite angles of a parallelogram are always congruent. Moreover, if a quadrilateral has ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of opposite congruent sides or  ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) pairs of opposite congruent angles, it is always a parallelogram.\n\n![](images/39/39ccf26333785f9ef47eed68a96bc40005a6ba025fdb6e250beefe14f10b043d.png)\n\n- A **rectangle** is a parallelogram where all angles are right.\n\n \n\n![](images/55/55a16e0ba93bf1f01d576cca558805aaea9631d1fa8e43b149509d4f8eb4c834.png)\n\n A rectangle where all sides are congruent is a **square**.\n\n![](images/a0/a04b2a0e86be8b085c5e3fc906d5aa66dfea45c033d52cd4746031202a7d427d.png)\n\n- A **rhombus** is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent. The opposite angles of a rhombus are also congruent.\n\n![](images/d3/d3588d7cbe8f285198df3909df3c03fdec04f53a3f75769e46a0ab4283fa27b3.png)\n\n A rhombus where all angles are congruent is a **square**.\n\n \n\n![](images/94/941872050d5407bcd9cb532e318276adccff802230ed854a2d292666fc0096fc.png)\n\n- A **kite** is a quadrilateral with two pairs of congruent sides that are adjacent to each other.\n\n![](images/11/116e323d81ee190c0f69b4d7cdcefe9f0952da883b0d6d3c6d4036119794baa7.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110831 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
